{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9ff40de0229b9263", "type": "tool_result", "content": "y and analyze why perceptual metrics and existing quality models fail in the low and ultra-low bitrate coding regime, and cast codec evaluation as source-conditioned comparative reasoning in CodecArena . ❑ (2) We propose Facet-GRPO , a facet-grounded preference learning scheme that combines holistic pairwise alignment with automatically derived facet-direction anchors, yielding hack-resistant and interpretable per-facet quality scores without requiring human per-facet labels. ❑ (3) To address the lack of data for quality assessment of low and ultra-low bitrate video coding, we build a fully automatic pipeline that produces the 1,500-group CodecArena-1K training set and CodecArena-Bench , the first human-ranked benchmark for this task. ❑ (4) On CodecArena-Bench, CodecArena aligns closely with human preference on source-disjoint content, surpassing both perceptual metrics and prior vision-language evaluators. Related Work Evolution of Video Compression Video compression has long been driven by the rate-distortion trade-off. Traditional hybrid codecs, from H.264/AVC (Wiegand et al. 2003 ) and HEVC (Sullivan et al. 2012 ) to VVC (Bross et al. 2021 ) and AV1 (Han et al. 2021 ) , combine prediction, transform coding, quantization, in-loop filtering, and entropy coding to exploit spatial and temporal redundancy, yet at low bitrates information must still be discarded or approximated. Neural codecs instead replace hand-designed modules with learned components, from end-to-end predictive systems (Lu et al. 2019 ; Agustsson et al. 2020 ) to the DCVC family, which advances conditional coding through decoded feature contexts (Li et al. 2021 , 2023 ; Jia et al. 2025 ) . In parallel, generative and perceptual codecs introduce adversarial losses and generative priors to produce visually plausible reconstructions under severe rate constraints, from generative image (Mentzer et al. 2020 ) and video (Mentzer et al. 2022 ) compression to recent video-oriented methods such as GVC (Chen et al. 2025 ) , GLC-Video (Qi et al. 2025 ) , and GNVC-VD (Mao et al. 2026 ) . Diffusion priors push perceptual quality still higher at extremely low bitrates for both images (Xu et al. 2025 ; Jia et al. 2026 ) and video (Xue et al. 2025 ; Zheng et al. 2026 ) . This shift from signal-level toward learned and perceptual reconstruction broadens compression artifacts beyond blocking, blur, and ringing; low-bitrate reconstruction may now also alter object identity, semantic consistency, fine structure, and temporal consistency. Quality Assessment for Video Coding Objective metrics remain the main protocol for comparing codecs. Full-reference metrics such as PSNR, SSIM (Wang et al. 2004 ) , and MS-SSIM (Wang et al. 2003 ) , together with perceptual metrics such as LPIPS (Zhang et al. 2018 ) , DISTS (Ding et al. 2022 ) , and VMAF (Li et al. 2016 ) , improve correlation with human judgment. Nevertheless, they penalize visually acceptable changes while missing semantic or identity-level failures, and they are applied as feature distances rather than as source-conditioned reasoning over what content should be preserved. More recently, multimodal large language models and vision-language models (VLMs) have moved beyond scalar scores toward interpretable, reasoning-based evaluation (Wu et al. 2024a ; You et al. 2024 ; Ge et al. 2025 ) , and reward models trained on large preference datasets capture fine-grained human preferences for generation (He et al. 2024 ; Xu et al. 2026 ; Wang et al. 2025 ) . Casting quality understanding as reinforcement learning, Q-Insight (Li et al. 2025 ) and VQ-Insight (Zhang et al. 2026 ) show that reward-driven reasoning yields generalizable evaluators. These methods, however, target natural, user-generated, or AI-generated content rather than codec-induced distortions, and do not compare a source video against multiple reconstructions from heterogeneous traditional, neural, and generative codecs. CodecArena is designed for exactly this comparative, source-conditioned setting. Methodology Preliminaries Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) (Shao et al. 2024 ) is a reinforcement learning framework for aligning large language and vision-language models. Unlike PPO (Schulman et al. 2017 ) , which relies on a separate value network to estimate returns, GRPO removes the explicit critic and instead derives advantages from the relative quality of a group of responses sampled for the same query, reducing both compute overhead and the instability of a learned value function. Given a query q q , GRPO samples N N candidate responses { o 1 , … , o N } \\{o_{1},\\dots,o_{N}\\} from the old policy π θ old \\pi_{\\theta_{\\text{old}}} , each assigned a scalar reward r i r_{i} by task-specific reward functions. The relative advantage is obtained by normalizing the rewards within the group: A ^ i = r i − mean ​ ( { r 1 , … , r N } ) std ​ ( { r 1 , … , r N } ) . \\hat{A}_{i}=\\frac{r_{i}-\\text{mean}(\\{r_{1},\\dots,r_{N}\\})}{\\text{std}(\\{r_{1},\\dots,r_{N}\\})}. (1) The policy is then updated to favor high-advantage responses under a clipped objective with a KL regularization term: 𝒥 G ​ R ​ P ​ O ​ ( θ ) \\displaystyle\\mathcal{J}_{GRPO}(\\theta) = 𝔼 [ q ∼ Q , o i ∼ π θ old ​ ( o | q ) ] { min [ ρ i A ^ i , \\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{[q\\sim Q,o_{i}\\sim\\pi_{\\theta_{\\text{old}}}(o|q)]}\\left\\{\\min\\left[\\rho_{i}\\hat{A}_{i},\\right.\\right. (2) clip ( ρ i , 1 − δ , 1 + δ ) A ^ i ] − β ⋅ 𝔻 KL [ π θ ∥ π ref ] } , \\displaystyle\\left.\\left.\\operatorname{clip}(\\rho_{i},1-\\delta,1+\\delta)\\hat{A}_{i}\\right]-\\beta\\cdot\\mathbb{D}_{\\mathrm{KL}}[\\pi_{\\theta}\\|\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}]\\right\\}, where ρ i = π θ new ​ ( o i ∣ q ) / π θ old ​ ( o i ∣ q ) \\rho_{i}=\\pi_{\\theta_{\\text{new}}}(o_{i}\\mid q)/\\pi_{\\theta_{\\text{old}}}(o_{i}\\mid q) , δ \\delta bounds the per-step update, and β \\beta weights the KL penalty against a frozen reference policy π ref \\pi_{\\text{ref}} . As GRPO is agnostic to the reward form, its effectiveness hinges on the design of the reward functions. Figure 2: Our coding-oriented preference data curation pipeline. Stage 1 re-encodes natural source videos with traditional, neural, and generative codecs and curates the reconstructions into comparison groups. Stage 2 scores every group automatically by fusing the VLM-ensemble judgments with objective signals. Stage 3 assembles CodecArena-1K from the automatic labels and refines CodecArena-Bench with weighted human rankings. Motivations of Our CodecArena At low and ultra-low bitrates, generative codecs synthesize visually sharp reconstructions that score well under LPIPS and DISTS yet corrupt semantically critical content, with drifting identities, illegible characters, and flicker across frames. Figure 1 shows a concrete case in which both metrics prefer a generative reconstruction that has already lost the person’s identity and made the on-screen text unreadable. Because they aggregate feature distances over the whole frame, these metrics overlook such localized failures; because they operate frame by frame, they also miss temporal incoherence. Many generative codecs also adopt an LPIPS-based term in their rate-distortion objective, so evaluating with the same insensitive metric lets a codec inflate its score without preserving the source. This motivates CodecArena, a framework that, conditioned on the source video, judges how faithfully each reconstruction preserves the original in semantics, structure, and temporal consistency. Compression degradations also differ fundamentally from the synthetic or in-the-wild distortions studied by VLM-based quality assessment such as Q-Insight (Li et al. 2025 ) . They are shaped by rate constraints, prediction, and codec-specific tools, couple spatial distortion with temporal behavior, and are even less understood for generative coding, where part of the reconstruction is hallucinated. Reliable quality assessment for the coding scenario is therefore essential, which CodecArena is built to provide. Coding-oriented Preference Data Curation Existing video quality datasets target natural, user-generated, or AI-generated distortions and overlook codec-induced degradations, especially the hallucinated artifacts that generative coding produces at low bitrate; they also rarely provide source-conditioned labels over several reconstructions of the same content. To fill this gap, the three-stage pipeline in Figure 2 builds two resources: CodecArena-1K , a fully automatically labeled training corpus of 1,500 source-conditioned comparison groups, and CodecArena-Bench , a human-verified benchmark of 80 held-out source videos. Figure 3: Word clouds of CodecArena-1K (a) and CodecArena-Bench (b). The two datasets share no source content, supporting fair evaluation on held-out content, while each spans diverse subjects, scenes, and objects. (1) Multi-codec re-encoding and group curation. We collect natural source clips from five complementary datasets, Vimeo-90K (Xue et al. 2019 ) , DAVIS 2019 (Caelles et al. 2019 ) , BVI-AOM (Nawała et al. 2024 ) , OpenVid (Nan et al. 2025 ) , and Xiph.org test sequences (Xiph.Org Foundation 2026 ) , spanning content that stresses faces, text, motion, and texture, with training and benchmark clips drawn from disjoint sources to evaluate generalization to held-out content. Each source is re-encoded by all three codec families, HEVC (Sullivan et al. 2012 ) and VVC (Bross et al. 2021 ) , the neural DCVC-DC (Li et al. 2023 ) and DCVC-RT (Jia et al. 2025 ) , and the generative GLC-Video (Qi et al. 2025 ) . To our knowledge GLC‑Video is the only generative video codec with publicly released implementation, as the authors provide an official codebase with both image and video compression support. Reconstructions from these codecs form comparison groups that balance content, codec family and rate, so that evaluations hinge on subtle semantic and temporal differences rather than gross distortion. (2) Quality annotation. Every group is scored automatically. A vision-la", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "9badee02-c83c-4450-a6b4-7910771c5324", "uuid": "e5d93a74-4dd6-4d34-b425-ef29c2dcbb0b", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T12:58:20.156Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "y and analyze why perceptual metrics and existing quality models fail in the low and ultra-low bitrate coding regime, and cast codec evaluation as source-conditioned comparative reasoning in CodecArena . ❑ (2) We propose Facet-GRPO , a facet-grounded preference learning scheme that combines holistic pairwise alignment with automatically derived facet-direction anchors, yielding hack-resistant and interpretable per-facet quality scores without requiring human per-facet labels. ❑ (3) To address the lack of data for quality assessment of low and ultra-low bitrate video coding, we build a fully automatic pipeline that produces the 1,500-group CodecArena-1K training set and CodecArena-Bench , the first human-ranked benchmark for this task. ❑ (4) On CodecArena-Bench, CodecArena aligns closely with human preference on source-disjoint content, surpassing both perceptual metrics and prior vision-language evaluators. Related Work Evolution of Video Compression Video compression has long been driven by the rate-distortion trade-off. Traditional hybrid codecs, from H.264/AVC (Wiegand et al. 2003 ) and HEVC (Sullivan et al. 2012 ) to VVC (Bross et al. 2021 ) and AV1 (Han et al. 2021 ) , combine prediction, transform coding, quantization, in-loop filtering, and entropy coding to exploit spatial and temporal redundancy, yet at low bitrates information must still be discarded or approximated. Neural codecs instead replace hand-designed modules with learned components, from end-to-end predictive systems (Lu et al. 2019 ; Agustsson et al. 2020 ) to the DCVC family, which advances conditional coding through decoded feature contexts (Li et al. 2021 , 2023 ; Jia et al. 2025 ) . In parallel, generative and perceptual codecs introduce adversarial losses and generative priors to produce visually plausible reconstructions under severe rate constraints, from generative image (Mentzer et al. 2020 ) and video (Mentzer et al. 2022 ) compression to recent video-oriented methods such as GVC (Chen et al. 2025 ) , GLC-Video (Qi et al. 2025 ) , and GNVC-VD (Mao et al. 2026 ) . Diffusion priors push perceptual quality still higher at extremely low bitrates for both images (Xu et al. 2025 ; Jia et al. 2026 ) and video (Xue et al. 2025 ; Zheng et al. 2026 ) . This shift from signal-level toward learned and perceptual reconstruction broadens compression artifacts beyond blocking, blur, and ringing; low-bitrate reconstruction may now also alter object identity, semantic consistency, fine structure, and temporal consistency. Quality Assessment for Video Coding Objective metrics remain the main protocol for comparing codecs. Full-reference metrics such as PSNR, SSIM (Wang et al. 2004 ) , and MS-SSIM (Wang et al. 2003 ) , together with perceptual metrics such as LPIPS (Zhang et al. 2018 ) , DISTS (Ding et al. 2022 ) , and VMAF (Li et al. 2016 ) , improve correlation with human judgment. Nevertheless, they penalize visually acceptable changes while missing semantic or identity-level failures, and they are applied as feature distances rather than as source-conditioned reasoning over what content should be preserved. More recently, multimodal large language models and vision-language models (VLMs) have moved beyond scalar scores toward interpretable, reasoning-based evaluation (Wu et al. 2024a ; You et al. 2024 ; Ge et al. 2025 ) , and reward models trained on large preference datasets capture fine-grained human preferences for generation (He et al. 2024 ; Xu et al. 2026 ; Wang et al. 2025 ) . Casting quality understanding as reinforcement learning, Q-Insight (Li et al. 2025 ) and VQ-Insight (Zhang et al. 2026 ) show that reward-driven reasoning yields generalizable evaluators. These methods, however, target natural, user-generated, or AI-generated content rather than codec-induced distortions, and do not compare a source video against multiple reconstructions from heterogeneous traditional, neural, and generative codecs. CodecArena is designed for exactly this comparative, source-conditioned setting. Methodology Preliminaries Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) (Shao et al. 2024 ) is a reinforcement learning framework for aligning large language and vision-language models. Unlike PPO (Schulman et al. 2017 ) , which relies on a separate value network to estimate returns, GRPO removes the explicit critic and instead derives advantages from the relative quality of a group of responses sampled for the same query, reducing both compute overhead and the instability of a learned value function. Given a query q q , GRPO samples N N candidate responses { o 1 , … , o N } \\{o_{1},\\dots,o_{N}\\} from the old policy π θ old \\pi_{\\theta_{\\text{old}}} , each assigned a scalar reward r i r_{i} by task-specific reward functions. The relative advantage is obtained by normalizing the rewards within the group: A ^ i = r i − mean ​ ( { r 1 , … , r N } ) std ​ ( { r 1 , … , r N } ) . \\hat{A}_{i}=\\frac{r_{i}-\\text{mean}(\\{r_{1},\\dots,r_{N}\\})}{\\text{std}(\\{r_{1},\\dots,r_{N}\\})}. (1) The policy is then updated to favor high-advantage responses under a clipped objective with a KL regularization term: 𝒥 G ​ R ​ P ​ O ​ ( θ ) \\displaystyle\\mathcal{J}_{GRPO}(\\theta) = 𝔼 [ q ∼ Q , o i ∼ π θ old ​ ( o | q ) ] { min [ ρ i A ^ i , \\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{[q\\sim Q,o_{i}\\sim\\pi_{\\theta_{\\text{old}}}(o|q)]}\\left\\{\\min\\left[\\rho_{i}\\hat{A}_{i},\\right.\\right. (2) clip ( ρ i , 1 − δ , 1 + δ ) A ^ i ] − β ⋅ 𝔻 KL [ π θ ∥ π ref ] } , \\displaystyle\\left.\\left.\\operatorname{clip}(\\rho_{i},1-\\delta,1+\\delta)\\hat{A}_{i}\\right]-\\beta\\cdot\\mathbb{D}_{\\mathrm{KL}}[\\pi_{\\theta}\\|\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}]\\right\\}, where ρ i = π θ new ​ ( o i ∣ q ) / π θ old ​ ( o i ∣ q ) \\rho_{i}=\\pi_{\\theta_{\\text{new}}}(o_{i}\\mid q)/\\pi_{\\theta_{\\text{old}}}(o_{i}\\mid q) , δ \\delta bounds the per-step update, and β \\beta weights the KL penalty against a frozen reference policy π ref \\pi_{\\text{ref}} . As GRPO is agnostic to the reward form, its effectiveness hinges on the design of the reward functions. Figure 2: Our coding-oriented preference data curation pipeline. Stage 1 re-encodes natural source videos with traditional, neural, and generative codecs and curates the reconstructions into comparison groups. Stage 2 scores every group automatically by fusing the VLM-ensemble judgments with objective signals. Stage 3 assembles CodecArena-1K from the automatic labels and refines CodecArena-Bench with weighted human rankings. Motivations of Our CodecArena At low and ultra-low bitrates, generative codecs synthesize visually sharp reconstructions that score well under LPIPS and DISTS yet corrupt semantically critical content, with drifting identities, illegible characters, and flicker across frames. Figure 1 shows a concrete case in which both metrics prefer a generative reconstruction that has already lost the person’s identity and made the on-screen text unreadable. Because they aggregate feature distances over the whole frame, these metrics overlook such localized failures; because they operate frame by frame, they also miss temporal incoherence. Many generative codecs also adopt an LPIPS-based term in their rate-distortion objective, so evaluating with the same insensitive metric lets a codec inflate its score without preserving the source. This motivates CodecArena, a framework that, conditioned on the source video, judges how faithfully each reconstruction preserves the original in semantics, structure, and temporal consistency. Compression degradations also differ fundamentally from the synthetic or in-the-wild distortions studied by VLM-based quality assessment such as Q-Insight (Li et al. 2025 ) . They are shaped by rate constraints, prediction, and codec-specific tools, couple spatial distortion with temporal behavior, and are even less understood for generative coding, where part of the reconstruction is hallucinated. Reliable quality assessment for the coding scenario is therefore essential, which CodecArena is built to provide. Coding-oriented Preference Data Curation Existing video quality datasets target natural, user-generated, or AI-generated distortions and overlook codec-induced degradations, especially the hallucinated artifacts that generative coding produces at low bitrate; they also rarely provide source-conditioned labels over several reconstructions of the same content. To fill this gap, the three-stage pipeline in Figure 2 builds two resources: CodecArena-1K , a fully automatically labeled training corpus of 1,500 source-conditioned comparison groups, and CodecArena-Bench , a human-verified benchmark of 80 held-out source videos. Figure 3: Word clouds of CodecArena-1K (a) and CodecArena-Bench (b). The two datasets share no source content, supporting fair evaluation on held-out content, while each spans diverse subjects, scenes, and objects. (1) Multi-codec re-encoding and group curation. We collect natural source clips from five complementary datasets, Vimeo-90K (Xue et al. 2019 ) , DAVIS 2019 (Caelles et al. 2019 ) , BVI-AOM (Nawała et al. 2024 ) , OpenVid (Nan et al. 2025 ) , and Xiph.org test sequences (Xiph.Org Foundation 2026 ) , spanning content that stresses faces, text, motion, and texture, with training and benchmark clips drawn from disjoint sources to evaluate generalization to held-out content. Each source is re-encoded by all three codec families, HEVC (Sullivan et al. 2012 ) and VVC (Bross et al. 2021 ) , the neural DCVC-DC (Li et al. 2023 ) and DCVC-RT (Jia et al. 2025 ) , and the generative GLC-Video (Qi et al. 2025 ) . To our knowledge GLC‑Video is the only generative video codec with publicly released implementation, as the authors provide an official codebase with both image and video compression support. Reconstructions from these codecs form comparison groups that balance content, codec family and rate, so that evaluations hinge on subtle semantic and temporal differences rather than gross distortion. (2) Quality annotation. Every group is scored automatically. A vision-la", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-82a9c6ff4c494c44", "type": "tool_result", "content": "nguage judge, an ensemble of Kimi-K2.6 (Moonshot AI 2026 ) and Opus-4.7 (Anthropic 2026 ) for reliability, compares the reconstructions pairwise over seven-frame filmstrips and rates each side from 0 to 1 along five facets, identity, objects, text, texture, and temporal consistency, under a rubric that penalizes hallucinated or identity-shifting content over honest blur. We fuse this VLM score with seven objective signals spanning face identity, full- and no-reference quality, text legibility, and temporal consistency into a single quality score, normalizing per group before a weighted average that also enforces the within-codec rate monotonicity the VLM alone does not guarantee. (3) Grading and dataset assembly. Ranking each group by this score into ordinal grades yields CodecArena-1K , the 1,500-group corpus that supervises CodecArena without manual annotation. For CodecArena-Bench , 15 annotators, 10 video-coding experts and 5 non-domain viewers, independently re-rank every group, and we aggregate their orderings under an expertise-weighted scheme into human labels over 80 videos spanning diverse scenes, resolutions, and codecs at medium-low to ultra-low bitrate. As the word clouds in Figure 3 confirm, both datasets cover diverse subjects, scenes, and objects despite disjoint sources, so evaluation on held-out content remains representative. The complete pipeline, the exact objective signals, and the fusion weights are given in the supplementary. Facet-GRPO Preference Learning Figure 4: Overview of CodecArena’s Facet-GRPO training. An Elastic Temporal Context Query presents the reference and two reconstructions at a variable temporal granularity, from a single still image to a multi-frame clip, together with the quality facets to be judged. The trainable policy reasons inside <think> and emits a per-facet Facet Verdict inside <answer> , regularized toward a frozen reference model by a KL term. Parsing the verdict yields the side-level qualities that the Facet-GRPO trainer scores with the Preference, Facet-Anchored, and Calibration rewards. We post-train the vision-language judge with Facet-GRPO, a group-relative scheme tailored to source-conditioned codec comparison, summarized in Figure 4 . A query q = ( R , A , B ) q=(R,A,B) presents a reference video R R and two reconstructions A A and B B as filmstrips, and the policy response o o ends with a structured verdict that assigns a score s X k ​ ( o ) ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] s_{X}^{k}(o)\\in[0,1] to each side X ∈ { A , B } X\\in\\{A,B\\} and facet k ∈ ℱ = { identity, objects, text, texture, temporal } k\\in\\mathcal{F}=\\{\\text{identity, objects, text, texture, temporal}\\} , with facets absent from the reference marked NA. The side-level quality s ¯ X ​ ( o ) \\bar{s}_{X}(o) is the mean over present facets and the preferred side is w ​ ( o ) = arg ⁡ max X ⁡ s ¯ X ​ ( o ) w(o)=\\arg\\max_{X}\\bar{s}_{X}(o) . The curated data supplies three supervision signals per comparison, namely Bradley–Terry latent qualities b A , b B b_{A},b_{B} encoding the holistic preference, an objective composite quality y X ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] y_{X}\\in[0,1] per side, and per-facet objective signals y X k y_{X}^{k} . The central risk in this setting is reward hacking. Since s ¯ X \\bar{s}_{X} averages over facets, a policy can win a comparison by inflating one salient facet, such as synthesized texture, while a face or a caption is corrupted, mirroring the very failure of LPIPS-style feedback. Facet-GRPO therefore instantiates the reward r i r_{i} of Eq. (1) as complementary terms that constrain the verdict at both the holistic and the facet level, optimized under an elastic temporal context. Reasoning and format rewards. Following the think-then-answer convention of Q-Insight (Li et al. 2025 ) , the policy reasons inside <think> tags and emits the score lines inside <answer> tags. A format term r fmt r_{\\mathrm{fmt}} grants a small constant when the facet scores of both sides are well formed, and a reasoning term r think r_{\\mathrm{think}} grants full credit only when a substantive reasoning block precedes a well-formed answer, reduced credit when reasoning is present but the answer is malformed, and zero when reasoning is absent. Without r think r_{\\mathrm{think}} , GRPO gradually collapses the chain-of-thought into score-only outputs; with it, the verdict remains grounded in explicit visual evidence. Preference and calibration rewards. The Preference Reward aligns the model’s choice with the curated ranking through the Bradley–Terry probability r pref ​ ( o ) = σ ​ ( b w ​ ( o ) − b l ​ ( o ) ) , r_{\\mathrm{pref}}(o)=\\sigma\\!\\big(b_{w(o)}-b_{l(o)}\\big), (3) where l ​ ( o ) l(o) denotes the rejected side and σ \\sigma the logistic function; a confident pair thus rewards a correct call near 1 1 and a wrong one near 0 . The Calibration Reward matches the magnitude of the side-level quality to the objective composite, r reg ​ ( o ) = 1 − 1 2 ​ ( | s ¯ A ​ ( o ) − y A | + | s ¯ B ​ ( o ) − y B | ) . r_{\\mathrm{reg}}(o)=1-\\tfrac{1}{2}\\big(\\,|\\bar{s}_{A}(o)-y_{A}|+|\\bar{s}_{B}(o)-y_{B}|\\,\\big). (4) Together they fix the ordering and the scale of the verdict. Facet-anchored reward. The preference and calibration terms leave the facet decomposition under-determined, precisely the loophole that hacking exploits. The Facet-Anchored Reward closes it. Let 𝒟 ​ ( q ) = { k : | y A k − y B k | > ϵ } \\mathcal{D}(q)=\\{k:|y_{A}^{k}-y_{B}^{k}|>\\epsilon\\} collect the facets on which the objective signals separate the two sides unambiguously, with direction p k = sign ⁡ ( y A k − y B k ) p^{k}=\\operatorname{sign}(y_{A}^{k}-y_{B}^{k}) . We reward agreement with every such direction, r facet ​ ( o ) = 1 | 𝒟 ​ ( q ) | ​ ∑ k ∈ 𝒟 ​ ( q ) σ ​ ( γ ​ p k ​ ( s A k ​ ( o ) − s B k ​ ( o ) ) ) , r_{\\mathrm{facet}}(o)=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{D}(q)|}\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal{D}(q)}\\sigma\\!\\big(\\gamma\\,p^{k}\\,(s_{A}^{k}(o)-s_{B}^{k}(o))\\big), (5) where γ \\gamma sharpens the per-facet margin and a comparable facet left unscored contributes zero. Credit is shared across all separated facets, such that no single sub-score can carry the comparison. This term injects no human per-facet target value, only the direction in which the automatically derived signals already order the pair, so calibrated per-facet scoring emerges without human per-facet annotation. Elastic temporal context query. Rather than fixing the input format, we pose each comparison as an elastic temporal context query: at every training step we draw a frame count K ∈ { 1 , 3 , 5 , 7 } K\\in\\{1,3,5,7\\} and render the whole batch, reference and reconstructions alike, at that granularity. For K > 1 K>1 the query is a short clip and all five facets apply; for K = 1 K=1 it degenerates to a single decoded still, an image-level assessment in which the temporal facet is declared not scorable and removed from 𝒟 ​ ( q ) \\mathcal{D}(q) , while the remaining rewards operate unchanged. Treating temporal context as an elastic training variable rather than a fixed format yields a single judge that assesses codec quality from one still frame to a full clip. Overall objective. The terms combine into r i = r fmt + r pref + r reg + λ f ​ r facet + λ t ​ r think , r_{i}=r_{\\mathrm{fmt}}+r_{\\mathrm{pref}}+r_{\\mathrm{reg}}+\\lambda_{f}\\,r_{\\mathrm{facet}}+\\lambda_{t}\\,r_{\\mathrm{think}}, (6) where λ f \\lambda_{f} controls the facet-direction anchors and λ t \\lambda_{t} controls the reasoning bonus. The reward enters the group-relative advantage of Eq. (1) and is optimized with Eq. (2) over N N sampled verdicts, requiring neither a value network nor a learned reward model. Unlike Q-Insight, which regresses a holistic image score with auxiliary degradation labels, or VQ-Insight (Zhang et al. 2026 ) , which regresses dense opinion scores for a single generated video, Facet-GRPO is comparative and source-conditioned. It grounds decomposition through directional, automatically derived facet signals plus holistic preferences, yielding hack-resistant per-facet scores without human per-facet labels. Experiments Implementation Details We instantiate CodecArena on Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct (Bai et al. 2025 ) and Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct (Bai and others 2025 ) . The 1,500 groups of CodecArena-1K are split into 1,050 training and 450 held-out validation groups, and the judges are evaluated on the source-disjoint CodecArena-Bench. We set N = 8 N{=}8 , β = 1 × 10 − 3 \\beta{=}1\\times 10^{-3} , λ f = 0.6 \\lambda_{f}{=}0.6 , and λ t = 0.2 \\lambda_{t}{=}0.2 , and fine-tune each model for 2 epochs on 8 NVIDIA A800 GPUs with learning rate 1 × 10 − 6 1\\times 10^{-6} and effective batch size 128. Each query samples K ∈ { 1 , 3 , 5 , 7 } K\\in\\{1,3,5,7\\} . Quantitative Results No established benchmark exists for this regime, so we evaluate on the human-labeled, source-disjoint CodecArena-Bench . Each protocol is tested at K = 1 K{=}1 (image), K = 7 K{=}7 (trained clip length), and K = 14 K{=}14 (extrapolation). Baselines include PSNR, SSIM (Wang et al. 2004 ) , LPIPS (Zhang et al. 2018 ) , DISTS (Ding et al. 2022 ) , VMAF (Li et al. 2016 ) , Q-ReAlign-Pro-9B (Q-Future 2026 ) , DeQA-Score (You et al. 2025 ) , Q-Insight (Li et al. 2025 ) , and VQ-Insight (Zhang et al. 2026 ) . We additionally report a VLM Ensemble, the arithmetic mean of Opus-4.7 (Anthropic 2026 ) and Kimi-K2.6 (Moonshot AI 2026 ) scores. Q-Insight and VQ-Insight are developed on Qwen2.5-VL-7B (Bai et al. 2025 ) , while Q-ReAlign-Pro-9B is an improved Q-Align (Wu et al. 2024b ) built on Qwen3.5-9B (Qwen Team 2026 ) . Table 1: Score-based ranking on CodecArena-Bench . Best/second-best are bold / underlined . K = 1 K{=}1 K = 7 K{=}7 K = 14 K{=}14 Method SRCC ↑ \\uparrow PLCC ↑ \\uparrow SRCC ↑ \\uparrow PLCC ↑ \\uparrow SRCC ↑ \\uparrow PLCC ↑ \\uparrow Score-based metrics PSNR 0.687 0.817 0.808 0.876 0.813 0.876 SSIM (Wang et al. 2004 ) 0.753 0.833 0.829 0.892 0.841 0.891 LPIPS (Zhang et al. 2018 ) 0.798 0.817 0.723 0.758 0.748 0.763 DISTS (Ding et al. 2022 ) 0.661 0.722 0.658 0.729 0.669 0.724 VMAF (Li et al. 2016 ) 0.782 0.853 0.876 0.919 0.878 0.917 Vision-language evaluators VLM Ensemble 0.798 0.799 0.794 0.798 0.753 0.766 Q-ReAlign-Pro-9B (Q-Future 2026 ) 0.636 0.675 0.642 0.679 0.691 0.687 DeQA-Score (You et al. 2025 ) 0.511 0.650 0.483 0.647 0.461 0.632 Q-Insight (Li et al. 2025 ) 0.587 0.650 0.589 0.651 0.614 0.659 VQ-Insight (Zhang et al. 2026 ) 0.663 0.681 0.573 0.639 0.672 0.674 CodecArena (Qwen2.5-VL-7B) 0.871 0.906 0.902 0.912 0.906 0.908 Δ \\Delta vs. Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct +0.235 +0.228 +0.407 +0.365 +0.456 +0.413 CodecArena (Qwen3-VL-8B) 0.877 0.909 0.895 0.923 0.894 0.924 Δ \\Delta vs. Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct +0.161 +0.185 +0.190 +0.198 +0.199 +0.228 Score-based ranking. Each model scores one reconstruction against the reference, inducing a within-group ranking evaluated by SRCC and PLCC. Table 1 shows that VMAF is the strongest non-learned metric and remains competitive on PLCC, but the two CodecArena variants occupy the top SRCC positions at every granularity. LPIPS and DISTS fall behind PSNR and VMAF on SRCC, confirming that feature similarity diverges from human preference here. CodecArena also beats all language baselines, including the larger Q-ReAlign-Pro-9B, and maintains accuracy at K = 14 K{=}14 despite training only with K ≤ 7 K{\\leq}7 . Table 2: Pairwise preference accuracy on CodecArena-Bench . Best/second-best are bold / underlined . Accuracy (%) ↑ \\uparrow Method K = 1 K{=}1 K = 7 K{=}7 K = 14 K{=}14 Score-based metrics PSNR 80.8 85.8 86.7 SSIM (Wang et al. 2004 ) 83.3 87.9 88.9 LPIPS (Zhang et al. 2018 ) 82.9 79.6 78.9 DISTS (Ding et al. 2022 ) 73.8 75.0 76.1 VMAF (Li et al. 2016 ) 85.8 89.2 90.0 Vision-language evaluators VLM Ensemble 85.2 80.9 83.6 Q-ReAlign-Pro-9B (Q-Future 2026 ) 78.3 77.5 81.1 DeQA-Score (You et al. 2025 ) 73.4 72.1 73.9 Q-Insight (Li et al. 2025 ) 80.9 76.5 75.6 VQ-Insight (Zhang et al. 2026 ) 81.8 79.0 83.4 CodecArena (Qwen2.5-VL-7B) 89.5 92.7 92.3 CodecArena (Qwen3-VL-8B) 93.7 94.9 93.0 Pairwise pref", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "9badee02-c83c-4450-a6b4-7910771c5324", "uuid": "c397cd54-2447-4e3c-8763-1b46e1692d03", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T12:58:20.403Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "nguage judge, an ensemble of Kimi-K2.6 (Moonshot AI 2026 ) and Opus-4.7 (Anthropic 2026 ) for reliability, compares the reconstructions pairwise over seven-frame filmstrips and rates each side from 0 to 1 along five facets, identity, objects, text, texture, and temporal consistency, under a rubric that penalizes hallucinated or identity-shifting content over honest blur. We fuse this VLM score with seven objective signals spanning face identity, full- and no-reference quality, text legibility, and temporal consistency into a single quality score, normalizing per group before a weighted average that also enforces the within-codec rate monotonicity the VLM alone does not guarantee. (3) Grading and dataset assembly. Ranking each group by this score into ordinal grades yields CodecArena-1K , the 1,500-group corpus that supervises CodecArena without manual annotation. For CodecArena-Bench , 15 annotators, 10 video-coding experts and 5 non-domain viewers, independently re-rank every group, and we aggregate their orderings under an expertise-weighted scheme into human labels over 80 videos spanning diverse scenes, resolutions, and codecs at medium-low to ultra-low bitrate. As the word clouds in Figure 3 confirm, both datasets cover diverse subjects, scenes, and objects despite disjoint sources, so evaluation on held-out content remains representative. The complete pipeline, the exact objective signals, and the fusion weights are given in the supplementary. Facet-GRPO Preference Learning Figure 4: Overview of CodecArena’s Facet-GRPO training. An Elastic Temporal Context Query presents the reference and two reconstructions at a variable temporal granularity, from a single still image to a multi-frame clip, together with the quality facets to be judged. The trainable policy reasons inside <think> and emits a per-facet Facet Verdict inside <answer> , regularized toward a frozen reference model by a KL term. Parsing the verdict yields the side-level qualities that the Facet-GRPO trainer scores with the Preference, Facet-Anchored, and Calibration rewards. We post-train the vision-language judge with Facet-GRPO, a group-relative scheme tailored to source-conditioned codec comparison, summarized in Figure 4 . A query q = ( R , A , B ) q=(R,A,B) presents a reference video R R and two reconstructions A A and B B as filmstrips, and the policy response o o ends with a structured verdict that assigns a score s X k ​ ( o ) ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] s_{X}^{k}(o)\\in[0,1] to each side X ∈ { A , B } X\\in\\{A,B\\} and facet k ∈ ℱ = { identity, objects, text, texture, temporal } k\\in\\mathcal{F}=\\{\\text{identity, objects, text, texture, temporal}\\} , with facets absent from the reference marked NA. The side-level quality s ¯ X ​ ( o ) \\bar{s}_{X}(o) is the mean over present facets and the preferred side is w ​ ( o ) = arg ⁡ max X ⁡ s ¯ X ​ ( o ) w(o)=\\arg\\max_{X}\\bar{s}_{X}(o) . The curated data supplies three supervision signals per comparison, namely Bradley–Terry latent qualities b A , b B b_{A},b_{B} encoding the holistic preference, an objective composite quality y X ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] y_{X}\\in[0,1] per side, and per-facet objective signals y X k y_{X}^{k} . The central risk in this setting is reward hacking. Since s ¯ X \\bar{s}_{X} averages over facets, a policy can win a comparison by inflating one salient facet, such as synthesized texture, while a face or a caption is corrupted, mirroring the very failure of LPIPS-style feedback. Facet-GRPO therefore instantiates the reward r i r_{i} of Eq. (1) as complementary terms that constrain the verdict at both the holistic and the facet level, optimized under an elastic temporal context. Reasoning and format rewards. Following the think-then-answer convention of Q-Insight (Li et al. 2025 ) , the policy reasons inside <think> tags and emits the score lines inside <answer> tags. A format term r fmt r_{\\mathrm{fmt}} grants a small constant when the facet scores of both sides are well formed, and a reasoning term r think r_{\\mathrm{think}} grants full credit only when a substantive reasoning block precedes a well-formed answer, reduced credit when reasoning is present but the answer is malformed, and zero when reasoning is absent. Without r think r_{\\mathrm{think}} , GRPO gradually collapses the chain-of-thought into score-only outputs; with it, the verdict remains grounded in explicit visual evidence. Preference and calibration rewards. The Preference Reward aligns the model’s choice with the curated ranking through the Bradley–Terry probability r pref ​ ( o ) = σ ​ ( b w ​ ( o ) − b l ​ ( o ) ) , r_{\\mathrm{pref}}(o)=\\sigma\\!\\big(b_{w(o)}-b_{l(o)}\\big), (3) where l ​ ( o ) l(o) denotes the rejected side and σ \\sigma the logistic function; a confident pair thus rewards a correct call near 1 1 and a wrong one near 0 . The Calibration Reward matches the magnitude of the side-level quality to the objective composite, r reg ​ ( o ) = 1 − 1 2 ​ ( | s ¯ A ​ ( o ) − y A | + | s ¯ B ​ ( o ) − y B | ) . r_{\\mathrm{reg}}(o)=1-\\tfrac{1}{2}\\big(\\,|\\bar{s}_{A}(o)-y_{A}|+|\\bar{s}_{B}(o)-y_{B}|\\,\\big). (4) Together they fix the ordering and the scale of the verdict. Facet-anchored reward. The preference and calibration terms leave the facet decomposition under-determined, precisely the loophole that hacking exploits. The Facet-Anchored Reward closes it. Let 𝒟 ​ ( q ) = { k : | y A k − y B k | > ϵ } \\mathcal{D}(q)=\\{k:|y_{A}^{k}-y_{B}^{k}|>\\epsilon\\} collect the facets on which the objective signals separate the two sides unambiguously, with direction p k = sign ⁡ ( y A k − y B k ) p^{k}=\\operatorname{sign}(y_{A}^{k}-y_{B}^{k}) . We reward agreement with every such direction, r facet ​ ( o ) = 1 | 𝒟 ​ ( q ) | ​ ∑ k ∈ 𝒟 ​ ( q ) σ ​ ( γ ​ p k ​ ( s A k ​ ( o ) − s B k ​ ( o ) ) ) , r_{\\mathrm{facet}}(o)=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{D}(q)|}\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal{D}(q)}\\sigma\\!\\big(\\gamma\\,p^{k}\\,(s_{A}^{k}(o)-s_{B}^{k}(o))\\big), (5) where γ \\gamma sharpens the per-facet margin and a comparable facet left unscored contributes zero. Credit is shared across all separated facets, such that no single sub-score can carry the comparison. This term injects no human per-facet target value, only the direction in which the automatically derived signals already order the pair, so calibrated per-facet scoring emerges without human per-facet annotation. Elastic temporal context query. Rather than fixing the input format, we pose each comparison as an elastic temporal context query: at every training step we draw a frame count K ∈ { 1 , 3 , 5 , 7 } K\\in\\{1,3,5,7\\} and render the whole batch, reference and reconstructions alike, at that granularity. For K > 1 K>1 the query is a short clip and all five facets apply; for K = 1 K=1 it degenerates to a single decoded still, an image-level assessment in which the temporal facet is declared not scorable and removed from 𝒟 ​ ( q ) \\mathcal{D}(q) , while the remaining rewards operate unchanged. Treating temporal context as an elastic training variable rather than a fixed format yields a single judge that assesses codec quality from one still frame to a full clip. Overall objective. The terms combine into r i = r fmt + r pref + r reg + λ f ​ r facet + λ t ​ r think , r_{i}=r_{\\mathrm{fmt}}+r_{\\mathrm{pref}}+r_{\\mathrm{reg}}+\\lambda_{f}\\,r_{\\mathrm{facet}}+\\lambda_{t}\\,r_{\\mathrm{think}}, (6) where λ f \\lambda_{f} controls the facet-direction anchors and λ t \\lambda_{t} controls the reasoning bonus. The reward enters the group-relative advantage of Eq. (1) and is optimized with Eq. (2) over N N sampled verdicts, requiring neither a value network nor a learned reward model. Unlike Q-Insight, which regresses a holistic image score with auxiliary degradation labels, or VQ-Insight (Zhang et al. 2026 ) , which regresses dense opinion scores for a single generated video, Facet-GRPO is comparative and source-conditioned. It grounds decomposition through directional, automatically derived facet signals plus holistic preferences, yielding hack-resistant per-facet scores without human per-facet labels. Experiments Implementation Details We instantiate CodecArena on Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct (Bai et al. 2025 ) and Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct (Bai and others 2025 ) . The 1,500 groups of CodecArena-1K are split into 1,050 training and 450 held-out validation groups, and the judges are evaluated on the source-disjoint CodecArena-Bench. We set N = 8 N{=}8 , β = 1 × 10 − 3 \\beta{=}1\\times 10^{-3} , λ f = 0.6 \\lambda_{f}{=}0.6 , and λ t = 0.2 \\lambda_{t}{=}0.2 , and fine-tune each model for 2 epochs on 8 NVIDIA A800 GPUs with learning rate 1 × 10 − 6 1\\times 10^{-6} and effective batch size 128. Each query samples K ∈ { 1 , 3 , 5 , 7 } K\\in\\{1,3,5,7\\} . Quantitative Results No established benchmark exists for this regime, so we evaluate on the human-labeled, source-disjoint CodecArena-Bench . Each protocol is tested at K = 1 K{=}1 (image), K = 7 K{=}7 (trained clip length), and K = 14 K{=}14 (extrapolation). Baselines include PSNR, SSIM (Wang et al. 2004 ) , LPIPS (Zhang et al. 2018 ) , DISTS (Ding et al. 2022 ) , VMAF (Li et al. 2016 ) , Q-ReAlign-Pro-9B (Q-Future 2026 ) , DeQA-Score (You et al. 2025 ) , Q-Insight (Li et al. 2025 ) , and VQ-Insight (Zhang et al. 2026 ) . We additionally report a VLM Ensemble, the arithmetic mean of Opus-4.7 (Anthropic 2026 ) and Kimi-K2.6 (Moonshot AI 2026 ) scores. Q-Insight and VQ-Insight are developed on Qwen2.5-VL-7B (Bai et al. 2025 ) , while Q-ReAlign-Pro-9B is an improved Q-Align (Wu et al. 2024b ) built on Qwen3.5-9B (Qwen Team 2026 ) . Table 1: Score-based ranking on CodecArena-Bench . Best/second-best are bold / underlined . K = 1 K{=}1 K = 7 K{=}7 K = 14 K{=}14 Method SRCC ↑ \\uparrow PLCC ↑ \\uparrow SRCC ↑ \\uparrow PLCC ↑ \\uparrow SRCC ↑ \\uparrow PLCC ↑ \\uparrow Score-based metrics PSNR 0.687 0.817 0.808 0.876 0.813 0.876 SSIM (Wang et al. 2004 ) 0.753 0.833 0.829 0.892 0.841 0.891 LPIPS (Zhang et al. 2018 ) 0.798 0.817 0.723 0.758 0.748 0.763 DISTS (Ding et al. 2022 ) 0.661 0.722 0.658 0.729 0.669 0.724 VMAF (Li et al. 2016 ) 0.782 0.853 0.876 0.919 0.878 0.917 Vision-language evaluators VLM Ensemble 0.798 0.799 0.794 0.798 0.753 0.766 Q-ReAlign-Pro-9B (Q-Future 2026 ) 0.636 0.675 0.642 0.679 0.691 0.687 DeQA-Score (You et al. 2025 ) 0.511 0.650 0.483 0.647 0.461 0.632 Q-Insight (Li et al. 2025 ) 0.587 0.650 0.589 0.651 0.614 0.659 VQ-Insight (Zhang et al. 2026 ) 0.663 0.681 0.573 0.639 0.672 0.674 CodecArena (Qwen2.5-VL-7B) 0.871 0.906 0.902 0.912 0.906 0.908 Δ \\Delta vs. Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct +0.235 +0.228 +0.407 +0.365 +0.456 +0.413 CodecArena (Qwen3-VL-8B) 0.877 0.909 0.895 0.923 0.894 0.924 Δ \\Delta vs. Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct +0.161 +0.185 +0.190 +0.198 +0.199 +0.228 Score-based ranking. Each model scores one reconstruction against the reference, inducing a within-group ranking evaluated by SRCC and PLCC. Table 1 shows that VMAF is the strongest non-learned metric and remains competitive on PLCC, but the two CodecArena variants occupy the top SRCC positions at every granularity. LPIPS and DISTS fall behind PSNR and VMAF on SRCC, confirming that feature similarity diverges from human preference here. CodecArena also beats all language baselines, including the larger Q-ReAlign-Pro-9B, and maintains accuracy at K = 14 K{=}14 despite training only with K ≤ 7 K{\\leq}7 . Table 2: Pairwise preference accuracy on CodecArena-Bench . Best/second-best are bold / underlined . Accuracy (%) ↑ \\uparrow Method K = 1 K{=}1 K = 7 K{=}7 K = 14 K{=}14 Score-based metrics PSNR 80.8 85.8 86.7 SSIM (Wang et al. 2004 ) 83.3 87.9 88.9 LPIPS (Zhang et al. 2018 ) 82.9 79.6 78.9 DISTS (Ding et al. 2022 ) 73.8 75.0 76.1 VMAF (Li et al. 2016 ) 85.8 89.2 90.0 Vision-language evaluators VLM Ensemble 85.2 80.9 83.6 Q-ReAlign-Pro-9B (Q-Future 2026 ) 78.3 77.5 81.1 DeQA-Score (You et al. 2025 ) 73.4 72.1 73.9 Q-Insight (Li et al. 2025 ) 80.9 76.5 75.6 VQ-Insight (Zhang et al. 2026 ) 81.8 79.0 83.4 CodecArena (Qwen2.5-VL-7B) 89.5 92.7 92.3 CodecArena (Qwen3-VL-8B) 93.7 94.9 93.0 Pairwise pref", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bf5921260f5f62f2", "type": "tool_result", "content": "erence. Each model selects the better reconstruction from a randomly ordered A/B pair. Table 2 shows that CodecArena is first and second at every granularity; the Qwen3-VL-8B model reaches 93.7 % 93.7\\% , 94.9 % 94.9\\% , and 93.0 % 93.0\\% accuracy from K = 1 K{=}1 to K = 14 K{=}14 . VMAF’s strong PLCC does not translate into pairwise choice, and the language baselines collapse close comparisons to a single appearance impression, whereas CodecArena weighs per-facet differences. Ablation Study Table 3: Ablation of Facet-GRPO components , added cumulatively to Qwen3-VL-8B. The full model uses λ f = 0.6 \\lambda_{f}{=}0.6 ; metrics follow Table 1 . K = 1 K{=}1 K = 7 K{=}7 K = 14 K{=}14 Configuration SRCC ↑ \\uparrow PLCC ↑ \\uparrow SRCC ↑ \\uparrow PLCC ↑ \\uparrow SRCC ↑ \\uparrow PLCC ↑ \\uparrow Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct 0.716 0.724 0.705 0.725 0.695 0.696 + Preference reward 0.770 0.795 0.789 0.831 0.782 0.819 + Elastic context 0.770 0.822 0.841 0.861 0.794 0.832 + Calibration reward 0.844 0.872 0.894 0.905 0.884 0.897 + Facet-anchored (Ours) 0.877 0.909 0.895 0.923 0.894 0.924 Figure 5: Single-stimulus scoring on a 7 7 -frame (left) and 14 14 -frame (right) clip. CodecArena aligns with the human preference, while LPIPS, DISTS, and VQ-Insight do not. Figure 6: Pairwise preference at K = 1 K{=}1 . Although humans prefer B, only CodecArena aligns with this preference, while LPIPS, DISTS, and Q-Insight favor A. We ablate Facet-GRPO by cumulatively adding each reward component to the native Qwen3-VL-8B backbone in Table 3 . Preference supervision alone is insufficient, as it improves coarse ranking but still leaves the model vulnerable to under-specified reasoning and unstable facet scores. Elastic context, calibration, and facet anchoring each improve ranking, with calibration giving the largest jump and the final facet anchor further regularizing sub-scores. We treat this as evidence that automatic facet directions provide complementary structure beyond holistic preference supervision, while the supplementary hard-case audit directly measures their effect on facet-level reward hacking. More ablations are present in Appendix. Qualitative Results Figures 5 and 6 cover a clip and a single image with the same judge. LPIPS, DISTS, VMAF, and prior VLM evaluators fold these errors into one appearance score and may prefer the lower-distortion but unfaithful reconstruction, while CodecArena’s facet scores remain grounded by objective signals. More qualitative results are provided in the appendix. These cases clarify why the pairwise gains in Table 2 are larger than the score-correlation gains in Table 1 . Codec decisions often hinge on a localized but decisive failure: one reconstruction may be slightly sharper overall while changing a face, corrupting a caption, or introducing temporal drift. A single scalar metric can still correlate with broad quality trends and yet choose the wrong side in exactly these close comparisons. CodecArena instead compares the source and both reconstructions facet by facet, so the final preference is tied to the content that humans use to reject unfaithful generative outputs. The protocol also makes the reported gains conservative. Each benchmark group compares reconstructions of the same source, so color, composition, and semantic content are shared and the decision turns on what each codec preserves or fabricates. The held-out sources are disjoint from CodecArena-1K, and the K = 14 K{=}14 setting further tests a temporal context never seen during training. The remaining errors are therefore not dominated by easy cases such as obvious blur or severe blocking; they arise from fine trade-offs between honest softness and plausible but wrong detail. This is precisely where automatic metrics are most likely to reward generative sharpness, and where a source-conditioned facet judge is most useful. Conclusion We presented CodecArena, a source-conditioned VLM judge for low- and ultra-low-bitrate codec assessment. Facet-GRPO learns from holistic preferences while grounding fine-grained scores with automatic facet-direction anchors, avoiding human per-facet labels. This design targets the evaluation gap created by generative codecs: a reconstruction can look sharper and still be less faithful to the source. By separating identity, objects, text, texture, and temporal consistency, CodecArena makes such trade-offs explicit rather than hiding them in one appearance score. This also changes what codec progress means in the generative regime: quality is not only smoother pixels or lower feature distance, but preservation of the source information a viewer relies on. Together with CodecArena-1K and the 80-group human-ranked CodecArena-Bench, it aligns with human rankings more closely than perceptual metrics and prior VLM evaluators, especially when plausible reconstructions alter identity, text, or temporal consistency. We hope this benchmark and judge encourage codec research to report semantic fidelity alongside rate-distortion efficiency, making future codec comparisons more faithful to the failures viewers actually notice and less dependent on metrics that generative models can exploit. This matters as learned decoders increasingly synthesize missing content rather than merely compress transmitted detail. References E. Agustsson, D. Minnen, N. Johnston, J. Ballé, S. J. Hwang, and G. Toderici (2020) Scale-Space Flow for End-to-End Optimized Video Compression . In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR) , Cited by: Evolution of Video Compression . Anthropic (2026) Claude Opus 4.7 . Note: https://www.anthropic.com/claude Large language model. Accessed: 2026-06-08 Cited by: Coding-oriented Preference Data Curation , Quantitative Results . S. Bai, K. Chen, X. Liu, J. Wang, W. Ge, et al. (2025) Qwe